Subject: Pre-scales register operands to byte offsets

What this does
  Pre-scales register operands to byte offsets.

Why we need it
  The register backend stored every register operand as a register index and scaled it by the 16-byte register size at each use, so every handler carried a shift on the hot path.

What it adds
  The register access macros add the frame base instead of indexing and scaling, 78 direct `fr[...]` uses were rewritten to them, the encoder, verifier and narrow-operand selection scale once, the monolith's `RD_REG`/`RD_REG8` drop their shift, and the reader bounds `reg_count` by the widest representable register offset and validates each register operand as an aligned byte offset rather than as an index; which the old unshifted encoding made harmless and this one makes wrong, and now use `RD_REG8`.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes. The register blobs are regenerated in 9999. `BC_VERSION` is unchanged.
